ural evolution to continue after deformation has ceased [Data: Entities (284); Relationships (318)].\\n\\nThis establishes holding at temperature as an enabling condition rather than an independent restoration mechanism. Its importance comes from providing the thermal environment in which static recovery, static recrystallization, and metadynamic recrystallization can operate [Data: Entities (254, 284); Relationships (318, 339)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Hot working initiates the deformation and restoration sequence\",\"explanation\":\"Hot working is the deformation processing of steels at elevated temperatures. The supplied data associate it with dynamic restoration and describe its effects as part of a broader process involving both dynamic and static restoration [Data: Entities (276); Relationships (311)].\\n\\nThe relationship to hot working conditions further connects the process to the reference context titled “Deformation under hot working conditions,” indicating that the community concerns processing performed under specified elevated-temperature deformation conditions [Data: Entities (1108); Relationships (1601)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Dynamically restored structures remain subject to later change\",\"explanation\":\"Dynamically restored structures are produced during deformation and are described as thermodynamically unstable. When the material is subsequently held at temperature, these structures are modified, linking deformation-stage restoration to post-deformation evolution [Data: Entities (313); Relationships (375)].\\n\\nThis relationship explains why the community is a sequential process network rather than a collection of isolated concepts: structures formed during hot working provide the state that is later acted upon by holding at temperature and static restoration [Data: Relationships (311, 318, 375)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Static recovery is one component of static restoration\",\"explanation\":\"Static recovery is identified as one of the static-restoration 